//! member: true
//!
//! std.hex — base-16 encoding and decoding. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **The byte sequence is `List[int]`, and that is an interim with a
//! filing behind it.** The sprint contract spells these functions over
//! `bytes`, the type `std.bytes` describes. That module has code as of
//! sc09 — eight of its nine functions, over exactly this currency — and it
//! still describes a `Bytes` TYPE that does not exist: a byte-width element
//! type that can hold invalid UTF-8 (F-0018/F-0057). So the currency here
//! is unchanged and so is the interim: `List[int]` with every element in
//! 0..255 — documented, checked at the boundary, and trivially replaced by
//! `Bytes` when `Bytes` exists, since every signature keeps its shape
//! (F-0035). `std.bytes` is now the module to reach for when a program
//! wants to LOOK at those bytes rather than encode them.
//!
//! What that used to cost was one function, and sc10 paid it:
//! **`encode_str` is here now.** Hex-encoding text is the commonest use
//! of this module in any language and it was missing for four sprints
//! because nothing could read a `str`'s bytes (F-0035); `s.bytes()` can,
//! on every lane (s37, and `std.bytes.from_str` wraps it), so the
//! function is one delegation. Its inverse is NOT here and will not be
//! until bytes can become text again (F-0057) — `encode_str`'s own doc
//! argues that asymmetry, because §11 pairs every encoder with its
//! decoder and this is the one place the pairing is refused.
//!
//! One name note: `encode_str` and not `encode`, because wolf has no
//! overloading and `encode(b: List[int])` owns the short name. It goes to
//! the byte form deliberately — that is the operation the round-trip
//! property is stated over.
//!
//! **Round-tripping is the doctrine** (the sprint contract's word): every
//! encoder here ships beside its decoder, `decode(encode(b))` is `b` for
//! every byte sequence, and `tests/hex/round_trip.lu` holds the property
//! over the shared adversarial fixtures rather than over a happy path.
//!
//! Decoding is TOTAL over arbitrary UTF-8 input, and at the sc12 pin it is
//! total for a simpler reason than the one it shipped with. sc03's
//! inversion — probe the input with an ASCII literal this module owns,
//! never slice the caller's string at a guessed offset — is what made
//! `decode("é")` a `parse` miss rather than a `bounds` trap while the
//! language had no byte accessor (F-0018). s77 gives the digit probe the
//! byte itself (`s.bytes()[0]`, one load rather than up to thirty-two
//! `starts_with` calls), and a byte cannot be a partial code point, so the
//! totality argument is now "a byte is a byte" and the answers are
//! unchanged member for member. `tests/hex/decode_misses.lu` is what holds
//! them to that.

/// The value of the hex digit `s` starts with, or -1. Case-insensitive.
/// Private.
///
/// **It reads one byte now, and that is s77's doing.** sc03's inversion
/// said: probe the input with a literal you own, because slicing the
/// caller's string at a guessed offset can trap. The probe cost up to
/// thirty-two `starts_with` calls per character, and it bought totality.
/// The byte view buys the same totality for one load — a byte is a byte, a
/// non-ASCII lead byte is 195 and not a digit — and it reads the
/// receiver's own storage rather than a copy.
///
/// The first byte is taken by ITERATING and returning, not by indexing:
/// `s.bytes()[0]` is refused by wolfc's checked tier (F-0071), and a shape
/// that costs a lane is not one std writes. An empty `s` runs the loop zero
/// times and falls through to the miss, which is the answer the
/// `starts_with` probe gave it too.
fn digit_of(s: str) -> int {
    for c in s.bytes() {
        if c >= 48 && c <= 57 {
            return c - 48
        }
        if c >= 97 && c <= 102 {
            return c - 87
        }
        if c >= 65 && c <= 70 {
            return c - 55
        }
        return 0 - 1
    }
    0 - 1
}

/// The two lowercase hex digits of one byte. Private; traps `assert` on a
/// value outside 0..255, which is this module's one caller-contract
/// check.
fn byte_digits(b: int) -> str {
    if b < 0 {
        assert(false)
    }
    if b > 255 {
        assert(false)
    }
    let alphabet = "0123456789abcdef"
    let hi = b / 16
    let lo = b % 16
    let high_digit = alphabet[hi..hi + 1]
    let low_digit = alphabet[lo..lo + 1]
    "{high_digit}{low_digit}"
}

/// `b` as lowercase hexadecimal, two digits per byte, no separators and
/// no `0x` prefix. The empty sequence encodes to the empty string.
///
/// Traps `assert` when an element is outside 0..255: the caller promised
/// bytes and did not deliver them, which is a contract violation and not
/// a datum (§2's trap rule). Total otherwise: no error row.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var b = List[int]()
/// (mut b).push(0)
/// (mut b).push(15)
/// (mut b).push(255)
/// hex.encode(b) == "000fff"
/// var empty = List[int]()
/// hex.encode(empty) == ""
/// ```
pub fn encode(b: List[int]) -> str {
    var out = ""
    let n = b.len
    var i: int = 0
    while i < n {
        let pair = byte_digits(b[i])
        out = "{out}{pair}"
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

/// `encode` with uppercase digits (`"000FFF"`). The uppercasing is the
/// implementation's `upper` over ASCII hex digits, where no Unicode
/// case-mapping question arises. Same `assert` trap on a non-byte.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var b = List[int]()
/// (mut b).push(171)
/// (mut b).push(205)
/// hex.encode_upper(b) == "ABCD"
/// ```
pub fn encode_upper(b: List[int]) -> str {
    let lower_text = encode(b)
    lower_text.upper()
}

/// `s` decoded from hexadecimal; `parse` when the length is odd or any
/// character is not a hex digit. Upper and lower case both decode, and
/// they may be mixed. The empty string decodes to the empty sequence.
///
/// The tag is `parse` — sc03's "these bytes are not a value of that
/// type" — and not the `hex` the sprint contract spells, for a reason
/// worth knowing before naming any tag: a row tag that shares a name with
/// a module in scope at the raise site resolves to the MODULE and rides
/// out of the function as a value, silently, with no diagnostic and no
/// `else` firing (F-0036). `std.hex` raising `hex` was the shape that
/// found it. The two miss cases an even more precise row would separate —
/// odd length and bad digit — wait for sc06's payload conventions rather
/// than being spelled as two bare tags.
///
/// Total: every input either decodes or misses — no traps, including on
/// non-ASCII input, which is a miss like any other non-digit.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let b = hex.decode("00ff") else List[int]()
/// b.len == 2
/// let bad = hex.decode("0f0") else List[int]()
/// bad.len == 0
/// let mixed = hex.decode("AbCd") else List[int]()
/// mixed.len == 2
/// let unicode = hex.decode("é") else List[int]()
/// unicode.len == 0
/// ```
pub fn decode(s: str) -> List[int] ! {parse} {
    var out = List[int]()
    var rest = s
    while rest.len > 0 {
        let hi = digit_of(rest)
        if hi < 0 {
            return parse
        }
        rest = rest[1..]
        if rest.len == 0 {
            return parse
        }
        let lo = digit_of(rest)
        if lo < 0 {
            return parse
        }
        rest = rest[1..]
        (mut out).push(hi * 16 + lo)
    }
    out
}

/// The UTF-8 bytes of `s`, hex-encoded — lowercase, two digits per byte,
/// no separators, no `0x` prefix.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. The empty string encodes to the empty
/// string. Every wolf `str` is well-formed UTF-8 by definition, so there
/// is no input this can refuse and no encoding parameter to get wrong.
///
/// **The census row this closes.** Hex-encoding text is the commonest use
/// of a hex encoder anywhere and this module shipped without it for four
/// sprints, because nothing in the language could read a `str`'s bytes
/// (F-0035). s37's `s.bytes()` can, on every lane, and the gap was a debt
/// rather than a wall after sc09 measured it.
///
/// **The body was `encode(s.bytes())` until sc12 and is a walk now.**
/// Passing the view to `encode` is exactly the position s77 materializes
/// in — an argument — so the delegation copied the whole string into a
/// `List[int]` before rendering a digit. Consuming the view here costs the
/// duplication of one four-line loop and no longer allocates a list per
/// call. The digits still come from `byte_digits`, so there is one
/// rendering and the two entry points cannot drift; what is duplicated is
/// the LOOP, not the answer.
///
/// The `assert` `encode` documents is unreachable through this door: every
/// element of a `str`'s byte view is a byte by construction.
///
/// **There is no `decode_str`, and that is a filing rather than an
/// omission** (§11's encoder/decoder pairing rule, refused honestly):
/// hex text decodes to bytes and nothing in the language turns bytes back
/// into a `str` — no `from_utf8`, no `char`, no `strbuf.push_byte`
/// (F-0057). `decode` plus `std.bytes.to_str` is the round trip, and
/// `std.bytes.to_str` is itself still a contract. So this module promises
/// the FORWARD direction only for text, and says so here rather than
/// shipping an ASCII-only inverse that would refuse the text people
/// actually hex-encode.
///
/// Uppercase is `encode_str(s).upper()`; there is no `encode_upper_str`,
/// because a second name for a call anyone can write is a second thing to
/// keep in agreement (§1).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// hex.encode_str("") == ""
/// hex.encode_str("wolf") == "776f6c66"
/// hex.encode_str("é") == "c3a9"
/// hex.encode_str("\n") == "0a"
/// ```
pub fn encode_str(s: str) -> str {
    var out = ""
    for b in s.bytes() {
        let pair = byte_digits(b)
        out = "{out}{pair}"
    }
    out
}
